From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:53:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Keep incomplete bodies out of the HTTP cache

The HTTP cache stores a response when its headers arrive and shares its
body with the fetch that receives it. When the body failed with an error
other than a cancellation, net still marked it as done, so the cache
kept the truncated body as a complete response and could answer later
requests with it. A cancelled body was emptied and marked as done before
the response was marked as aborted, so a lookup in between could answer
with an empty body, and a range cut from a stored response did not share
its aborted flag. A fetch that waited for a body still being received
got the incomplete body when the receiving fetch was aborted, and waited
forever when that fetch ended in a network error of its own, such as a
failed integrity check, because then only responses with an empty body
were woken up. And the cache never removed the entries of aborted
fetches, so every cancelled fetch left one more unusable entry in the
list of responses for its URL; media elements cancel a fetch on every
seek.

Mark the response as aborted when its body fails or is cancelled, before
the body is replaced. Constructing a response from the cache, or
refreshing it after a 304, checks the flag while holding the body,
ranges cut from a stored response share its flag, and the refresh skips
aborted responses. When the receiving fetch ends, wake every fetch that
waits for a complete or aborted body, whatever its status; one that
found the response by a cache lookup and whose body was aborted now
fetches from the network instead, or fails if it is only-if-cached. A
fetch that waits for the body through the refresh of a 304 is unchanged:
it still ends with an empty body when the receiving fetch is aborted.
Remove the entries of aborted fetches that nobody waits for when their
fetch ends and whenever a response is stored for the same URL, and the
URL once it has no entries left. The fetch that receives a failed body
still gets it as before, and only the cache reads the aborted flag of a
response.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: unit tests that ranges cut from a stored response share its
aborted flag, that revalidation skips aborted responses, and that the
cache removes aborted entries when it stores a response unless a fetch
waits for them; fetch tests that a truncated response is neither stored
nor served, that a cancelled fetch leaves neither an entry nor its URL
in the cache, that a fetch waiting for an aborted response fetches it
from the network, and that one waiting for a response whose fetch failed
its integrity check is woken up. `CancellationListener::cancel` is now
public, and the cache has test-only counts of stored responses and of
waiting fetches.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 403 tests pass. Each new test fails when its part of the fix is reverted: without the shared flag, a range keeps a flag of its own; without the skip, revalidation uses the aborted response; without the network fallback, the waiting fetch delivers the empty body of the aborted one; without waking every waiting fetch, the one waiting for the response that failed its integrity check never ends; without removing the URL, a cancelled fetch leaves it in the cache; and, checked on the first revision, the truncated response is stored and served without the aborted flag on errors, the aborted entries stay without their removal when a fetch ends or when a response is stored, and a waited-for entry is removed without the check for waiting fetches. Holding the body while checking the flag closes a race that no deterministic test reproduces. Playing, reloading and playing again a 90 s, 42 MB video as in the previous patch, VmRSS after the first play / at the end: 311/378, 328/388 and 329/389 MiB with both patches, 323/384 and 314/383 with the previous one alone, no measurable difference. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` of fetch/http-cache, fetch/range, fetch/api/basic and xhr: all 619 tests as expected; html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements as in the previous patch.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written and tested by the agent, reworked after a review by another agent)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
